WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.300
This is a shard from a pot

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.400
about 10,000 years old.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.200
It was found by an archaeologist

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.580
at a Chinese site called Jiahu.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:12.620
Which is a really interesting archaeological site.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.340
It has some of the oldest evidence
of rice being eaten as food.

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:16.780
It has some of the oldest musical instruments.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.360
It has some of the oldest written language.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:19.780
It's a really rich site.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:21.560
This pot when they found it

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:23.800
had some weird residue inside it.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.480
Which eventually made its way to an analytical chemist

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:29.439
named Patrick McGovern; he's at Penn.

00:00:29.439 --> 00:00:30.640
McGovern ran a whole bunch of tests:

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:33.000
Feigl spot test, gas chromatography,

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.440
mass spectroscopy, UV beams, shot lasers at it.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:38.900
Did everything he could in his lab.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:43.180
And eventually found the ingredients

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.340
of that residue.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.649
So you found three things. He found

00:00:47.649 --> 00:00:50.140
essentially oils from beeswax

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:50.698
which only comes from one place

00:00:50.698 --> 00:00:52.220
you get that from honey.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:53.890
He found evidence that rice

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:56.439
had been pulverized in the pot and

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:59.460
he found tartrates, tartaric acid.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.359
So for anywhere else at any other time that

00:01:01.359 --> 00:01:03.910
would probably have meant grapes which are

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:05.540
really high in that stuff.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:07.480
But while there were some wild grapes in China

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.510
10,000 years ago, there weren't

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:13.000
that many and they weren't vitis vinifera

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.740
which is the only species that we use

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:15.620
to make wine from.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:17.860
What they probably were more likely is hawthorn fruit

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:19.920
which is--I asked him what

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:20.500
that would taste like.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.440
He said it was like a chalky apple, basically.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:26.410
But it was fruit. And what McGovern

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:28.720
realized he had--he didn't find ethanol,

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.240
I should say. As i mentioned it's really volatile.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:33.040
It's evanescent. You never find evidence of the

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:34.600
actual booze. You just find kind of the

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:37.029
evidence of the evidence. But McGovern

00:01:37.029 --> 00:01:38.800
put all that stuff together in his head and

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.080
as he says you'd have a hard time not letting

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.280
that ferment if you mix all those things and left in a pot.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:44.760
It would want to ferment on its own

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:46.940
just because of yeast and the environment.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:51.940
This pot held the Mother Eve of booze.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:55.540
This is humanity's oldest known

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.880
intentionally alcoholic beverage. 10,000 years.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.840
Think of the ingredients. It's rice like in sake, right?

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:04.580
It's honey like in mead.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.700
It's fruit like in a wine. All mixed together.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.110
This is the oldest evidence that we have

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:13.180
of us trying to make something good to drink.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:18.620
I asked him if I could hold it.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:23.140
it's just in his office, it's on his shelf.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:25.520
But I asked him if I could hold it.
And he got very nervous.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.560
And he took out nitrile gloves.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:30.280
I put the gloves on, but I got to hold on to it.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.900
I've never held anything that old before.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:34.160
It's very profound.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:35.300
It's a connection to somebody else who just

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.220
tried to make some stuff, you know?

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:39.480
A homebrewer, right?

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:43.580
But that's how long we've been doing this at least, right?

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:45.680
harnessing this natural process

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.200
to do what we want it to do.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.380
This is us domesticating yeast

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:50.811
while yeast domesticates us.

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:51.820
Please give me more sugar

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:55.000
so i can make you this thing that you like.

